Data-driven Circuit Discovery
Research (8/2)
This paper (arXiv:2605.09129) studies whether circuit discovery methods actually find general “task circuits,” or only dataset-specific ones. Existing methods are hypothesis-driven: define a task, build a dataset, discover one circuit. The paper shows those assumptions fail — small dataset changes can yield low-overlap circuits with poor cross-dataset faithfulness, and mixed-task datasets can still produce one high-faithfulness circuit that mixes distinct mechanisms. They propose Data-driven Circuit Discovery (DCD): cluster examples by how the model processes them, then discover one circuit per cluster. In short: let the model’s internal mechanisms define the scope of each circuit, not human task labels.

Overview
Motivation / gap the paper identifies (Section 1–2)
Circuit discovery tries to explain how LMs implement a task by finding a circuit: a sparse computational subgraph responsible for the behavior.
Existing work shares a hypothesis-driven workflow: define a task → build a representative dataset → discover a single circuit → interpret it as the task mechanism.
That workflow assumes (i) the dataset adequately represents the task, and (ii) the model uses one circuit for the whole task.
Prior work already hints this is shaky: LMs can use multiple mechanisms for arithmetic, factual recall, entity binding, syntactic code generation, and IOI.
The open question: what is the true scope of a discovered circuit — the whole task, or only a subset of examples?
Study design, 3 RQs (Section 2–3)
RQ1: Do hypothesis-driven methods discover general task circuits? (Section 4)
RQ2: What if the dataset contains examples involving distinct mechanisms? (Section 5)
RQ3: Can we discover circuits without making these hypotheses? → DCD (Section 6)
Tasks (Section 3)
IOI (Indirect Object Identification): e.g. “When Mary and John went to the store, John gave an apple to” → predict Mary.
Entity Binding: list of (entity, container) pairs → predict entity for a queried container.
Arithmetic Addition: e.g. “10 + 50 =” → predict 60 (single-token operands/results).
Sequence Completion: repeated subsequence with final repetition truncated → predict next token (induction-style).
Models tested (Section 3)
GPT-2 Small (124M), Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct. Chosen for size/family diversity; only evaluated on tasks where each model is reasonably accurate.
Metrics (Section 3)
Faithfulness: how well circuit C recovers full-model behavior, f(C,G;m) = [m(C)−m(∅)] / [m(G)−m(∅)], using logit difference between correct vs counterfactual labels. 1 = matches full model; 0 = matches empty-circuit baseline; can go outside [0,1].
Jaccard similarity on edges: structural overlap between circuits, |E_i ∩ E_j| / |E_i ∪ E_j|.
Circuit size often reported as a fraction of edges kept (e.g. 0.05 = top 5%).
Less summarized repeat ↓
RQ1. Do hypothesis-driven methods discover general task circuits? (Section 4, Table 1, Figure 2)
They define a “general task circuit” as one that stays faithful under distribution shifts that preserve task semantics (same input–output relation, different surface form).
They build variants along three axes: complexity (e.g. 2-person vs 3-person IOI), syntax (e.g. passive vs active), domain (e.g. names vs letter labels).
Discover a circuit on each variant with EAP-IG; evaluate in-distribution vs cross-dataset faithfulness; also measure edge Jaccard overlap.
Result: high in-distribution faithfulness, but large cross-dataset drops — up to 79 percentage points at circuit size 0.05 (Table 1). Example: GPT-2 2-person IOI circuit ~73% in-distribution vs ~42% on 3-person at size 0.05 (31-point drop).
Mean pairwise edge overlap across same-task variants is only ~0.30–0.66; circuits differ structurally, not just in faithfulness.
Takeaway: hypothesis-driven methods recover dataset-specific circuits, not general task circuits. In-distribution faithfulness alone overstates explanation scope.
RQ2. What if the dataset mixes distinct mechanisms? (Section 5, Figure 3)
They mix Entity Binding and Arithmetic at ratios from EB=0.0 (pure arith) to EB=1.0 (pure EB) in steps of 0.1; discover one circuit per mixture with EAP-IG; evaluate on pure arith and pure EB test sets.
Independently discovered arith and EB circuits are mutually non-faithful at sparse sizes (near-zero cross-task faithfulness).
Once mixed, a single circuit can look highly faithful on both tasks. Example: at EB=0.5, ~89.8% faithfulness on arith and ~85.1% on EB at size 0.05. Adding just 10% arith into pure EB jumps arith faithfulness from ~5.8% to ~87.1%.
Edge overlap changes smoothly with mixture ratio (extremes share only ~26% top edges); no sharp boundary — the returned circuit is largely a function of dataset composition.
Takeaway: a high-faithfulness single circuit can mix distinct mechanisms; faithfulness on the discovery mix does not mean one coherent algorithm.
RQ3. Can we discover circuits without these hypotheses? → DCD (Section 6, Figures 4–5)
DCD pipeline: (1) per-example edge attribution vectors (e.g. EAP-IG); (2) reduce dim (PCA/SVD); (3) cluster examples (K-means / hierarchical / etc., choose K*); (4) discover one circuit per cluster.
Evaluated on all-task mixed datasets (and single-task mixed variants) vs baselines: EAP-IG / EAP / E-Act on full data, Random Edges, K-Representative, K-Random.
Metric for multi-circuit methods: best-of-K faithfulness (for each example, take the best of the K circuits).
DCD beats hypothesis-driven methods, especially at sparse sizes. Example: GPT-2 at size 0.05, DCD ~86% vs EAP-IG ~64% (+22 points).
On GPT-2 all-task mix, DCD finds coherent clusters (e.g. separate clusters for different IOI variants and for sequence completion); faithfulness heatmaps show block structure — each circuit is strong on its subset and near-zero elsewhere. K-Random can get similar best-of-K numbers but without coherent specialization.
Takeaway: dropping the single-circuit assumption yields multiple mechanism-specific circuits that are sparser and more faithful, with interpretable specialization.
Discussion / limitations the authors name themselves (Section 8)
Human “task” labels need not match the model’s internal organization; circuits should be scoped to clusters of similarly processed examples.
DCD still depends on attribution quality (inherits EAP-IG assumptions) and on clustering choices (K*, reduction, distance, algorithm).
Open problems: how to assign new test examples to clusters; how to interpret clusters that aren’t human-legible.

Key Findings
RQ1. Do hypothesis-driven methods discover general task circuits?
Tested on IOI, Entity Binding, Arithmetic, Sequence Completion across GPT-2, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.
Minor variants (complexity / syntax / domain) that preserve task semantics still produce circuits with low cross-dataset faithfulness (drops up to ~79 points) and modest edge overlap.
So “the IOI circuit” (etc.) is often dataset-specific, not task-general.
RQ2. Can a single circuit mix distinct mechanisms?
Yes. Mixing Entity Binding + Arithmetic into one discovery set yields one circuit that is highly faithful on both pure tasks, even though pure-task circuits are mutually non-faithful when discovered separately.
High faithfulness ≠ one coherent mechanism.
RQ3. Does DCD fix this?
DCD clusters by per-example attribution similarity, then discovers one circuit per cluster.
Recovers multiple specialized circuits; better sparse faithfulness than hypothesis-driven baselines; clusters align with coherent subsets (tasks / variants) rather than random mixes.
Suggested next steps (Discussion / broader agenda)
Treat circuit scope as data-driven (model computation), not fixed by human task names.
Characterize what each cluster means; improve test-time assignment to circuits.
Apply the same question beyond the paper’s four tasks — especially code generation.

Balanced Parentheses / Code Extension (this is the part that matters for Mohan’s project)
What it is
Prof. Yao’s scoped follow-up: is the DCD finding (different circuits/mechanisms for seemingly the same task) true for code generation as well?
Released DCD code: https://github.com/Ziyu-Yao-NLP-Lab/data-driven-circuit-discovery
Next experimental target: balanced parentheses tasks from https://github.com/Ziyu-Yao-NLP-Lab/failure-by-interference (examples needing 1–4 closing parentheses; related NeurIPS 2025 syntactic code-completion work).
Questions to explore
Does the model establish distinct circuits for different numbers of closing parentheses (1 vs 2 vs 3 vs 4)?
Does it establish distinct circuits depending on task context (e.g. closing for print / str vs tuple-style contexts)?
The gap that jumps out
DCD validated the multi-mechanism story on IOI / entity binding / arithmetic / sequence completion. Code generation (even simple parentheses completion) is the natural extension where humans may still label it as “one task” while the model uses different mechanisms — exactly the paper’s thesis applied to coding.

